&lt;p&gt;In a small town, there is a peculiar lottery system run by a man named Bob. The lottery machine is a random generator connected to a row of $n$ light bulbs. Whenever the random generator is activated, each of the $n$ lights is turned on with the probability of $\frac 1 2$, independently of its former state or the state of the other light bulbs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bob's friend Alice is a mathematician and they come up with two different lottery games, they call the &lt;dfn&gt;reciprocal lotteries&lt;/dfn&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
Both lotteries consist of $n$ rounds. Each round is started by choosing a number $k$ randomly between (and including) $1$ and $n$, with equal probability of $\frac 1 n$ for each number, while the possible win for that round is the reciprocal of $k$, that is $\frac 1 k$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In lottery A, Bob activates his random generator once in each round. If the number of lights turned on is the same as the previously chosen number $k$, Alice wins and gets $\frac 1 k$, otherwise she will receive nothing for that round. Alice's expected win after playing the total lottery A consisting of $n$ rounds is called $A_A(n)$. For example $A_A(6)=0.39505208$, rounded to $8$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each round in lottery B, after $k$ has been randomly selected, Bob keeps reactivating his random generator until exactly $k$ lights are turned on. After that Alice takes over and reactivates the random generator until she, too, has generated a pattern with exactly $k$ lights turned on. If this pattern is identical to Bob's last pattern, Alice wins and gets $\frac 1 k$, otherwise she will receive nothing. Alice's expected win after the total lottery B consisting of $n$ rounds is called $A_B(n)$. For example $A_B(6)=0.43333333$, rounded to $8$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let $\displaystyle T(m)=\sum_{n=1}^m (A_A(n)+A_B(n))$. For example $T(6)=7.58932292$, rounded to $8$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Find $T(987654321)$, rounded to $8$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

